P57 [Heading 1]: REVIEW OF RELATED LITERATURE
P58 [Heading 2]: 2.0 Introduction
P59 [Normal]: This chapter presents a comprehensive and structured review of the literature relevant to the development of a Personalized Multilingual Examination System for visually impaired candidates. Section 2.1 discusses the concept of Assistive Technology for visually impaired persons and its evolution within educational contexts. Section 2.2 reviews the landscape of Digital Assessment and Computer-Based Testing, including the challenges of making these systems accessible. Section 2.3 examines the technical domain of Multilingual Computing and Localization, with specific attention to the challenges of supporting indigenous Nigerian languages in accessible systems. Section 2.4 reviews the literature on Examination Security and Integrity and the known conflicts with assistive technology deployment. Section 2.5 discusses Inclusive Design and Universal Design for Learning as the overarching theoretical framework guiding this research. Section 2.6 addresses the broader challenges of accessible technology adoption in resource-constrained environments. Finally, Section 2.7 provides a detailed review of directly related prior works, identifies the research gap that this study addresses, and Section 2.8 presents the full table of reviewed literature.
P60 [Heading 2]: 2.1 Concept of Assistive Technology for Visually Impaired Persons
P61 [Normal]: Assistive technology for visual impairment is broadly defined as the range of devices, software, and specialized systems designed to enable persons with vision loss to independently access information, participate in educational activities, and perform tasks of daily living that would otherwise be inaccessible. The Assistive Technology Industry Association (2022) categorizes these technologies across several functional domains including access to printed information, access to electronic information, orientation and mobility, and support for activities of daily living. Within the educational context, the primary concern is the domain of electronic information access, which encompasses screen reader software, text-to-speech engines, refreshable Braille displays, and the emerging category of self-voicing applications. The integration of these technologies into educational assessment environments has become a critical area of inquiry as institutions transition from paper-based to digital examination platforms (Thompson et al., 2020).
P62 [Normal]: The educational implications of visual impairment are far-reaching and well-documented. Research by the American Foundation for the Blind (2021) indicates that students with visual disabilities encounter unique challenges in accessing educational materials, participating in classroom activities, and demonstrating their knowledge through conventional assessment methods. These challenges persist throughout the educational pipeline and have been associated with lower rates of educational attainment and subsequent economic disadvantage (NCES, 2022). The intersection of visual impairment with digital assessment design, therefore, represents not merely a technical problem but a matter of educational equity and social justice that demands systematic and principled engineering solutions.
P63 [Heading 3]: 2.1.1 Evolution of Assistive Technology for Visual Impairment
P64 [Normal]: The history of assistive technology for the visually impaired traces a trajectory from the mechanical Braille printing press introduced by Louis Braille in the nineteenth century to the sophisticated computational tools available in the twenty-first century. Early electronic assistive technologies emerged in the 1970s and 1980s in the form of text-to-speech synthesizers and simple screen reading programs that were designed for text-only operating environments. The transition to graphical user interfaces in the late 1980s and 1990s posed a significant challenge to these early tools, as the visual, icon-based interaction paradigm of graphical systems was fundamentally at odds with the text-based operation of early screen readers (Mynatt & Edwards, 2019).
P65 [Normal]: The response to this challenge gave rise to the modern generation of screen reader software. JAWS (Job Access With Speech), developed by Freedom Scientific and introduced in 1989, became the dominant commercial product in this space, employing a virtual buffer architecture to translate complex graphical interfaces into navigable textual representations. The release of NVDA (NonVisual Desktop Access) by NV Access in 2006 introduced a high-quality, open-source alternative that has since achieved widespread adoption, particularly in resource-constrained educational environments (NV Access, 2023). The development of these tools was enabled by advances in operating system accessibility APIs, including Microsoft Active Accessibility (MSAA), IAccessible2, and the more recent UI Automation framework, which provide the standardized communication bridge between applications and screen reader software (Microsoft, 2023).
P66 [Heading 3]: 2.1.2 Screen Readers and Self-Voicing Systems
P67 [Normal]: Modern screen readers represent complex technological ecosystems that interact with operating system accessibility APIs to construct a non-visual model of the user interface. This model is then conveyed to the user through speech synthesis, Braille output, or a combination of both modalities. The technical architecture of a screen reader involves continuous monitoring of application state changes, focus events, and content updates, translating all interface elements, including menus, dialog boxes, and dynamically updated content, into a form accessible through hearing or touch (Mynatt & Edwards, 2019).
P68 [Normal]: Self-voicing applications represent a distinct architectural approach in which text-to-speech functionality is embedded directly within the application itself rather than relying on an external screen reader. This architecture offers significant advantages in examination contexts where the presence of an external screen reader process may trigger security lockdown mechanisms. Saha, Sen, and Agarwal (2021) described the development of a self-voicing audio-based examination system for blind students in India, demonstrating that a 42% rate of difficulty with standard screen reader configurations among visually impaired candidates provides a compelling rationale for the self-voicing approach. By eliminating the dependency on external tools, self-voicing applications simplify the user experience and reduce the probability of security-accessibility conflicts during high-stakes assessments.
P69 [Heading 3]: 2.1.3 Text-to-Speech and Braille Display Technology
P70 [Normal]: Text-to-speech technology forms the foundation of audio-based accessibility in digital systems. Modern TTS engines utilize either formant synthesis, concatenative synthesis, or neural network-based synthesis to generate natural-sounding speech from digital text. The quality and naturalness of synthesized speech vary significantly across languages, with well-resourced languages like English benefiting from high-quality neural TTS voices while under-resourced languages such as Yoruba, Hausa, and Igbo are often served by lower-quality formant-based synthesizers that fail to accurately render tonal and prosodic features essential for meaning differentiation (Oladipupo & Oyelami, 2020). This disparity in TTS quality across languages represents one of the primary technical challenges addressed by this research.
P71 [Normal]: Refreshable Braille display technology has evolved in parallel with screen reader software to provide a tactile alternative for candidates who are deaf-blind or who prefer to read through touch. Modern devices offer configurations ranging from 40-cell to 80-cell units and interface with host computers via USB or Bluetooth, receiving Braille-translated text from the screen reader or application (Freedom Scientific, 2023). Research by Wittich and Watanabe (2021) found that individual preferences between audio and tactile reading modalities vary significantly among visually impaired users and are influenced by the nature of the reading task, underscoring the necessity of a personalized system that supports both output channels simultaneously.
P72 [Heading 3]: 2.1.4 Multilingual Assistive Technology
P73 [Normal]: The development of multilingual assistive technology represents one of the most underserved areas in the accessible computing landscape. While screen readers such as NVDA support over 50 interface languages through community-contributed translations (NV Access, 2023), the availability of high-quality TTS voices for indigenous African languages remains extremely limited. Oladipupo and Oyelami (2020) conducted pioneering research into the development of a Yoruba language screen reader, identifying the unique challenges of building TTS support for a tonal language in which pitch contour fundamentally alters lexical meaning. Their work demonstrated that generic multilingual TTS engines that approach all languages with a uniform phonological model consistently fail to produce intelligible or accurate audio output for tonal languages, producing outputs that can distort or invert the meaning of examination questions.
P74 [Normal]: The W3C Internationalization Activity provides authoritative guidelines for implementing multilingual digital systems, emphasizing proper Unicode character encoding, language tagging, and culturally appropriate interface design (W3C, 2023). The Unicode Consortium's UTF-8 encoding standard provides a universal character representation framework capable of accommodating virtually all of the world's writing systems, including the Latin-based extended character sets required for Yoruba diacritical marks and the Arabic-based script components used in Hausa writing (Unicode Consortium, 2022). Implementing these standards correctly in an examination system requires careful attention to TTS voice selection, prosodic modeling, and runtime language switching without disrupting the examination session or security state.
P75 [Heading 3]: 2.1.5 Challenges and Ethics in Assistive Technology Adoption
P76 [Normal]: While the technical potential of modern assistive technology is substantial, its ethical and practical implementation in educational settings is subject to significant constraints. The "digital divide" in accessible technology is pronounced in developing nations, where high-cost commercial tools such as JAWS (priced at several hundred US dollars annually) place robust accessibility solutions beyond the reach of the average student or institution (Freedom Scientific, 2023). Open-source alternatives such as NVDA partially address the cost barrier but are not always compatible with the security restrictions imposed by examination environments (Brajnik & Yesilada, 2021).
P77 [Normal]: Ethical deployment of assistive technology in examination contexts must also address the issue of measurement equivalence, ensuring that the accommodations provided to visually impaired candidates do not inadvertently advantage or disadvantage them relative to sighted peers. Thompson et al. (2020) conducted research validating that properly designed digital accommodations can maintain measurement equivalence while removing construct-irrelevant barriers related to visual ability. A "Human-in-the-Loop" design philosophy, where technology is used to support rather than replace human judgment and autonomy, is particularly important in ensuring that assistive examination systems are perceived as legitimate and trustworthy by both candidates and administrators (Holmes, 2019).
P78 [Heading 2]: 2.2 Digital Assessment and Computer-Based Testing
P79 [Normal]: The transition from paper-based to computer-based assessment represents one of the most significant and consequential shifts in educational measurement over the past two decades (Drasgow & Olson-Buchanan, 2019). This transformation has been driven by multiple complementary factors: the efficiency gains associated with automated scoring and immediate feedback, the enhanced security offered by randomized item banks, the ability to incorporate multimedia content, and the reduction of logistical overhead associated with paper-based administration. However, the COVID-19 pandemic, which compelled institutions worldwide to deploy digital assessment platforms under emergency conditions, exposed significant and previously underappreciated accessibility gaps in hastily adopted digital examination systems (Okonkwo & Ade-Ibijola, 2021).
P80 [Normal]: Computer-based testing presents a theoretically promising foundation for accessibility. Digital formats enable flexible content presentation, including text magnification, contrast adjustment, and text-to-speech conversion, that would be technically impossible in a paper-based format. However, the National Federation of the Blind (2022) found that a majority of blind students encountered significant technical difficulties with online examination platforms in practice, revealing a fundamental gap between the theoretical flexibility of digital formats and the practical accessibility of their deployed implementations.
P81 [Heading 3]: 2.2.1 Stakeholders in the Digital Assessment Environment
P82 [Normal]: The digital assessment ecosystem involves a diverse network of stakeholders whose requirements and perspectives must all be accommodated in a well-designed system. Examination administrators require confidence in the security and integrity of assessment results. Technology developers must balance competing requirements for security, accessibility, usability, and multilingual support. Candidates with visual impairments require reliable, independent, and linguistically appropriate access to examination content. Regulatory bodies and disability rights organizations, including the W3C and the World Blind Union, establish the technical and ethical standards to which all systems must conform. Understanding the needs and constraints of each stakeholder group is essential for designing an examination architecture that achieves the goal of inclusive assessment (Thompson et al., 2021).
P83 [Heading 3]: 2.2.2 The Flow of Information in Computer-Based Testing
P84 [Normal]: In a standard CBT system, information flows through four primary stages: content delivery, candidate interaction, response capture, and result generation. For sighted candidates, this flow is mediated entirely by visual interfaces, mouse navigation, and keyboard input. For visually impaired candidates, each stage of this flow must be reimagined around non-visual modalities. Content must be delivered through audio or tactile output; candidate interaction must be mediated through keyboard commands or switch access; response capture must provide auditory confirmation; and result generation must be conveyed through audio feedback (Mynatt & Edwards, 2019). When this non-visual information flow is not explicitly designed into the examination architecture from the outset, the retrofitting of accessibility becomes architecturally complex and often incomplete, resulting in a degraded candidate experience (Thompson et al., 2020).
P85 [Heading 3]: 2.2.3 Challenges in Accessible Digital Assessment
P86 [Normal]: The management of accessibility in digital assessment environments is beset by a series of interconnected challenges that individually impede and collectively prevent the equitable participation of visually impaired candidates.
P87 [Normal]: The Security-Accessibility Paradox: The most critical technical challenge is the fundamental incompatibility between examination security mechanisms and assistive technology. Lockdown browsers that prevent access to external applications also block the operating system APIs required by screen readers, creating a binary choice between examination integrity and candidate independence (Brajnik & Yesilada, 2021).
P88 [Normal]: Dependency on Human Intermediaries: In the absence of compatible digital solutions, institutions routinely assign human scribes to visually impaired candidates. This practice is resource-intensive, compromises candidate privacy and independence, and introduces the risk of interpretive error or unconscious bias, fundamentally undermining the objectivity of the assessment (World Blind Union, 2021).
P89 [Normal]: Linguistic Inaccessibility: The overwhelming English-language bias of digital examination platforms creates particular barriers for candidates in multilingual contexts. When a candidate who thinks and comprehends most naturally in Yoruba must navigate an English-only audio examination system, the assessment measures linguistic accommodation rather than subject-matter knowledge (Oladipupo & Oyelami, 2020).
P90 [Normal]: Internet Dependency: Most modern CBT platforms assume reliable internet connectivity, an assumption that fails in many rural and peri-urban areas of Nigeria and other developing nations. Offline-capable examination systems are essential for equitable deployment across diverse infrastructure contexts (UNESCO, 2022).
P91 [Heading 3]: 2.2.4 Traditional Assessment Practices for Visually Impaired Candidates
P92 [Normal]: Prior to the widespread adoption of digital assessment, visually impaired candidates were served through three primary modalities: oral examinations conducted by a human examiner, Braille-transcribed paper examinations, and examination access mediated by a human reader or scribe. Each of these approaches carries significant limitations. Oral examinations are resource-intensive and introduce inter-examiner variability. Braille transcription requires specialized expertise and is not available for all subject areas or examination types. Scribe-mediated examination fundamentally compromises candidate independence, as the candidate must verbalize responses and rely on the scribe for accurate transcription. While these traditional approaches have provided a degree of functional access, none is scalable, cost-effective, or fully consistent with the principles of independent assessment (Çetin, 2025).
P93 [Heading 3]: 2.2.5 Digital Technologies in Accessible Assessment
P94 [Normal]: The adoption of digital technologies creates unprecedented opportunities for transforming the accessibility of examination systems. Text-to-speech synthesis enables any text-based examination content to be delivered through audio. Unicode encoding provides the character representation infrastructure to support multilingual content rendering. Lightweight desktop application frameworks enable the development of secure, offline examination environments that do not depend on internet connectivity. Open-source accessibility APIs, including the IAccessible2 standard maintained by the Linux Foundation, provide a standardized communication layer between applications and assistive technology that developers can integrate natively rather than relying on external screen reader compatibility (Petrie & Kheir, 2019).
P95 [Normal]: The integration of these technologies requires careful architectural planning. A system that uses TTS for audio output, HID protocols for Braille display integration, Unicode for content encoding, and a custom kiosk-mode security implementation represents a substantial engineering undertaking. However, the modular architecture of modern desktop frameworks, including Electron and Python-based cross-platform toolkits, makes this integration feasible within the scope of academic research and development (Saha et al., 2021).
P96 [Heading 3]: 2.2.6 Role of Artificial Intelligence in Accessible Assessment Design
P97 [Normal]: Emerging artificial intelligence techniques offer promising complementary contributions to accessible examination design. Natural language processing models can improve the quality of TTS output by providing more accurate grapheme-to-phoneme conversion for complex or ambiguous text, including proper nouns and technical terminology that conventional TTS engines frequently mispronounce. AI-based speech recognition could ultimately enable fully voice-controlled examination interfaces where candidates enter responses through speech, eliminating the keyboard entirely (Yadav & Gupta, 2019). Additionally, adaptive examination systems driven by item response theory and AI-based item selection could dynamically adjust the difficulty and format of examination questions based on candidate performance, offering a more accurate and nuanced assessment of knowledge. While these AI-driven capabilities are identified as directions for future work, the conceptual framework they provide informs the design of the proposed system's personalization and delivery architecture (Sharma et al., 2025).
P98 [Heading 2]: 2.3 Multilingual Computing and Localization
P99 [Normal]: The support of multiple languages in computing systems encompasses a set of technical challenges related to character encoding, text rendering, linguistic processing, and text-to-speech synthesis. Unicode, specifically the UTF-8 encoding scheme, has emerged as the universal standard for representing text across virtually all of the world's writing systems (Unicode Consortium, 2022). For the purposes of this research, the relevant character sets include the extended Latin alphabet required for Yoruba (with its combination of tone marks, nasality indicators, and underdot characters), the Hausa orthography which blends standard Latin characters with hooked consonants, and the Igbo orthography with its combination of digraphs and high-frequency vowels. Ensuring that an examination system correctly renders, stores, retrieves, and vocalizes all characters in each of these orthographies simultaneously requires a robust Unicode implementation throughout the entire technology stack.
P100 [Normal]: Internationalization (i18n) and localization (l10n) are the complementary software engineering processes through which multilingual capability is achieved. Internationalization involves designing the software architecture to accommodate multiple languages without requiring fundamental changes to the codebase, while localization involves the actual translation of content and the adaptation of linguistic processing to each specific language. The W3C Internationalization Activity provides authoritative technical guidelines for both processes, including recommendations for language tagging, bidirectional text handling, and dynamic language switching (W3C, 2023).
P101 [Normal]: For audio-based accessibility, the most critical localization challenge is the development of accurate and intelligible TTS voices for each target language. Oladipupo and Oyelami (2020) demonstrated in their groundbreaking work on Yoruba language assistive technology that building TTS support for tonal languages requires specialized prosodic modeling that goes well beyond the capabilities of generic multilingual TTS engines. Yoruba employs three phonemic tones: high, mid, and low as well as nasality and vowel length distinctions that carry distinct lexical meaning. A TTS engine that fails to accurately render these prosodic features does not merely produce unnatural-sounding speech; it may produce speech that conveys a fundamentally different meaning from the written text, with direct and serious implications for examination validity. The proposed system addresses this challenge by integrating dedicated TTS engines for each language with language-specific acoustic models, rather than relying on a single multilingual engine that applies uniform phonological rules across all languages.
P102 [Heading 2]: 2.4 Examination Security and Integrity
P103 [Normal]: Examination security encompasses the complete set of measures designed to prevent malpractice, protect the confidentiality of test content, and ensure that examination outcomes accurately and validly reflect candidate knowledge and ability (Cizek, 2021). In the context of high-stakes digital examinations, where results have significant consequences for educational progression, professional certification, or employment, security concerns demand both technical and procedural solutions. The primary technical mechanism deployed in modern secure digital assessment is the lockdown browser, a specialized application that restricts the host computer to a single, examination-specific interface by disabling access to other applications, preventing screen capture, blocking internet access, and monitoring system-level activity during the examination session (Milone, 2020).
P104 [Normal]: The fundamental problem with lockdown browser technology from an accessibility perspective is its indiscriminate approach to disabling external software processes. Because screen readers rely on privileged access to operating system APIs specifically MSAA, IAccessible2, and UI Automation on Windows to read the state of the application interface, these APIs may be classified by a lockdown browser's security logic as potential attack vectors and blocked accordingly (Brajnik & Yesilada, 2021). The result is that the security mechanism that prevents an unscrupulous sighted candidate from accessing unauthorized resources simultaneously prevents a blind candidate from perceiving the examination content at all. Brajnik and Yesilada (2021) conducted a systematic evaluation of commercial lockdown browsers and found that this incompatibility was widespread and consistent, representing a fundamental design failure rather than an isolated technical oversight.
P105 [Normal]: Alternative approaches to examination security that do not carry the same accessibility implications include remote proctoring through video monitoring and AI-based behavioral analysis, secure physical testing centers with human supervision, and cryptographic response encryption combined with psychometric detection of anomalous response patterns (Milone, 2020). Each of these approaches has its own accessibility implications. The proposed system addresses the security challenge through a custom kiosk-mode implementation that operates at the application layer rather than the OS layer, preserving accessibility API access while restricting all other external software interaction. This design follows the architectural patterns proposed by Brajnik and Yesilada (2021) as the optimal approach to resolving the security-accessibility conflict in digital examination design.
P106 [Heading 2]: 2.5 Inclusive Design and Universal Design for Learning
P107 [Normal]: Inclusive design is a philosophy of design practice that considers the full range of human diversity encompassing ability, language, culture, gender, age, and other dimensions of human difference as constitutive rather than peripheral to the design problem (Holmes, 2019). The foundational insight of inclusive design is that designing for the margins, for those users whose needs differ most significantly from the assumed norm, produces solutions that improve the experience for everyone. The accessible keyboard controls developed for screen reader users, for example, are equally beneficial to power users who prefer keyboard navigation over mouse interaction. This principle is central to the design philosophy of the proposed system, where individual candidate needs, expressed through a personalization profile drive system behavior rather than a static, assumed-universal interface.
P108 [Normal]: Universal Design for Learning (UDL) applies inclusive design principles specifically to educational contexts, providing a framework for designing learning and assessment experiences that are effective and accessible for all learners from the outset rather than through retrospective accommodation (CAST, 2021). The UDL framework, developed by the Center for Applied Special Technology (CAST), emphasizes the provision of multiple means of engagement, multiple means of representation, and multiple means of action and expression, principles that are directly operationalized in the proposed system's support for audio, Braille, and personalized language output. Thompson et al. (2021) applied UDL principles specifically to large-scale assessment design, finding that proactively designed accessible assessments are more valid, more equitable, and less resource-intensive than accommodation-based approaches that retrofit accessibility to inaccessible original designs.
P109 [Normal]: The concept of personalization as a design principle extends the UDL framework in an important direction. Visually impaired candidates are not a homogeneous population; they differ in degree of vision loss, preferred assistive modality, language background, level of digital literacy, and prior experience with assistive technology (Saha et al., 2021; Wittich & Watanabe, 2021). A system designed around a single assumed profile of the "visually impaired user" will inevitably fail to serve significant portions of this diverse population effectively. The proposed system's personalization engine, which allows candidates to configure output language, modality, speech rate, and display preferences before the examination begins, operationalizes this insight into a concrete technical architecture.
P110 [Heading 2]: 2.6 Challenges of Accessible Technology Adoption in Digital Assessment
P111 [Normal]: While the theoretical case for accessible digital examination systems is well-established and the technical building blocks are increasingly available, the practical adoption of these systems in educational institutions particularly in developing nations faces a complex array of structural, technical, and socio-economic barriers that must be explicitly acknowledged and addressed in system design.
P112 [Heading 3]: 2.6.1 Infrastructure and Connectivity Constraints
P113 [Normal]: The most fundamental infrastructural challenge in the Nigerian examination context is the unreliability of internet connectivity, particularly in rural and peri-urban areas. The majority of modern digital examination platforms assume stable broadband connectivity, an assumption that fails consistently outside major urban centers. UNESCO (2022) documented that approximately 60% of sub-Saharan African educational institutions operate in environments with limited or no reliable internet access. This infrastructural reality necessitates that any examination system designed for broad deployment in Nigeria must be fully capable of offline operation, with all examination content, TTS voices, and security logic stored and executed locally on the host device. The proposed system's offline-first architecture directly responds to this constraint (Okonkwo & Ade-Ibijola, 2021).
P114 [Heading 3]: 2.6.2 Cost Barriers and Commercial Tool Dependency
P115 [Normal]: The financial barrier imposed by commercial assistive technology is one of the most significant and persistent factors limiting accessibility in developing-country examination environments. JAWS, the most widely adopted commercial screen reader, carries a license cost that is prohibitive for the vast majority of Nigerian educational institutions and individual candidates. The availability of free, open-source alternatives such as NVDA partially addresses this barrier but does not resolve the compatibility problems that arise when screen readers are used in conjunction with commercial lockdown browsers (Brajnik & Yesilada, 2021). A system architecture that integrates TTS and Braille support natively, eliminating the dependency on any external commercial assistive technology, is the most direct response to the cost barrier. By building accessibility into the examination application itself, the proposed system ensures that no additional software purchase is required for a visually impaired candidate to access the full functionality of the platform.
P116 [Heading 3]: 2.6.3 Socio-Technical and Digital Literacy Barriers
P117 [Normal]: Many existing accessible technology solutions are designed with an implicit assumption of prior digital literacy that is not universally present among visually impaired candidates in developing-country contexts. Screen reader software such as JAWS and NVDA have command vocabularies of hundreds of keyboard shortcuts that require sustained training to learn effectively. In the examination context, where a candidate's cognitive resources should be entirely focused on demonstrating knowledge rather than navigating technology, requiring proficiency with a complex assistive technology interface is itself a form of construct-irrelevant barrier (Thompson et al., 2020). The proposed system addresses this by designing an examination interface that requires no prior screen reader training, employing a minimal and consistent navigation structure with full audio confirmation of all interactions (Holmes, 2019).
P118 [Heading 3]: 2.6.4 Language Technology Resource Gaps
P119 [Normal]: The development of high-quality TTS voices for Nigerian languages is constrained by the fundamental resource gap in available linguistic data. Neural TTS models, which produce the most natural-sounding speech, require large corpora of professionally recorded speech data from native speakers. For under-resourced languages such as Yoruba, Hausa, and Igbo, such corpora are either unavailable or insufficient for training high-quality neural models (Oladipupo & Oyelami, 2020). The proposed system responds to this constraint by combining the best available open-source TTS engines for each language, specifically eSpeak-NG for formant-based synthesis with custom phoneme dictionaries for Yoruba tonal rules, with the option to integrate higher-quality neural voices as they become available through projects such as Mozilla Common Voice.
P120 [Heading 3]: 2.6.5 Institutional and Policy Barriers
P121 [Normal]: Beyond technical challenges, the adoption of accessible examination systems requires institutional will, policy support, and awareness of disability rights obligations among examination administrators. In many Nigerian institutional contexts, accessibility accommodations are treated as exceptional arrangements requiring individual requests rather than as baseline requirements of a properly designed examination system. This mindset, which places the burden of accommodation on the candidate rather than on the institution, conflicts directly with the UDL principle that inclusive design eliminates the need for individual accommodation by making the default system universally accessible (CAST, 2021; Thompson et al., 2021). Changing this institutional orientation requires not only technical solutions but also advocacy, policy development, and professional development for examination administrators.
P122 [Heading 2]: 2.7 Review of Related Works
P123 [Normal]: Petrie and Kheir (2019) evaluated the accessibility of several web-based examination platforms using automated testing tools and manual screen reader assessment. Their findings revealed that while major platforms had implemented basic accessibility features, significant barriers persisted in complex question types including drag-and-drop interactions and mathematical notation entry. The study contributed to knowledge by providing empirical evidence of the gap between standards-compliance on paper and genuine functional accessibility in practice. However, the research focused exclusively on web-based platforms, providing no insights into the design of offline, self-voicing, or multilingual accessible examination systems specific to the Nigerian context.
P124 [Normal]: Saha, Sen, and Agarwal (2021) described the development and pilot evaluation of an audio-based examination system specifically designed for blind students in India. Their system employed the eSpeak TTS engine for audio output and achieved positive user feedback. Critically, their finding that 42% of visually impaired candidates experienced difficulties using standard screen reader configurations with existing examination platforms provides empirical motivation for the self-voicing architecture adopted in the present study. The research contributed a working prototype demonstrating the technical feasibility of the self-voicing approach. However, the system lacked Braille display integration, security mechanisms, and any multilingual support beyond English and Hindi, leaving a gap in coverage for the Nigerian multilingual examination context.
P125 [Normal]: McDonald and Crandell (2020) described a system providing synchronized audio and Braille output for science assessments specifically designed for deaf-blind users. Their work demonstrated the value of multimodal output in examination accessibility, showing that some content, particularly mathematical and scientific notation, is more accurately conveyed through Braille than through audio alone. The research contributed empirically validated multimodal design patterns that directly inform the Braille display integration component of the proposed system. However, the system was designed for a narrow subject domain and English language only, without security integration or multilingual support.
P126 [Normal]: Yadav and Gupta (2019) developed a voice-based examination system that incorporated both speech output and speech recognition for response entry, demonstrating the technical feasibility of a fully voice-controlled examination interface. Their system was designed for Indian languages, addressing a multilingual accessibility gap that directly parallels the gap this research targets for Nigerian languages. However, speech recognition accuracy in noisy environments remained a critical unresolved challenge, and the system lacked Braille support and a robust security framework applicable to high-stakes Nigerian examination environments.
P127 [Normal]: Brajnik and Yesilada (2021) conducted the most directly relevant study to this research, performing a systematic evaluation of lockdown browser compatibility with screen reader software. Their findings confirmed that most commercial lockdown solutions create substantial barriers for visually impaired candidates by blocking the operating system accessibility APIs required for screen reader operation. They proposed architectural design patterns for secure browsers that preserve accessibility API access while restricting unauthorized system interaction patterns that directly inform the kiosk-mode architecture of the proposed system. However, the research produced no working prototype system, offered no multilingual support, and was conducted in a commercial testing context that does not reflect the resource constraints of Nigerian educational institutions.
P128 [Normal]: Oladipupo and Oyelami (2020) conducted pioneering research into the development of a Yoruba language screen reader, providing detailed technical documentation of the challenges involved in building TTS support for a tonal African language. Their work involved developing custom phoneme dictionaries and prosodic rules to handle Yoruba's three-tone system, demonstrating that generic TTS engines consistently produce inaccurate and sometimes meaning-inverting output for tonal languages. The study contributed the foundational technical knowledge required for the present research's approach to Yoruba TTS integration. However, the resulting system was not integrated into any examination platform and provided no support for Hausa or Igbo, leaving a comprehensive Nigerian multilingual examination system as an open research challenge.
P129 [Normal]: Wittich and Watanabe (2021) examined reading speed and comprehension performance among blind students using refreshable Braille displays compared to audio output. Their findings provided empirical support for the provision of both output modalities in accessible examination design, demonstrating that neither audio nor Braille is universally superior and that individual preferences are influenced by task type, familiarity, and prior experience. This work directly informs the personalization module's design, validating the decision to support both audio and Braille as first-class output modalities with equal functional status in the proposed system.
P130 [Normal]: Thompson et al. (2021) applied Universal Design for Learning principles systematically to large-scale assessment design, providing a comprehensive evidence-based framework for proactively accessible examination development. Their work demonstrated that UDL-informed assessment design produces outcomes that are simultaneously more valid for candidates with disabilities and more equitable in their measurement properties than accommodation-based approaches. This framework provides the theoretical foundation for the proposed system's design philosophy and directly informs the inclusive-by-design principle that guides all architectural decisions.
