P181 [Normal]: CHAPTER THREE
P182 [Normal]: Research Methodology and System Design
P183 [Heading 2]: 3.1 Introduction
P184 [Normal]: This chapter provides a comprehensive description of the methods, procedures, and technical frameworks employed to achieve the objectives of this research. The primary focus of this study is to bridge the accessibility gap in digital examination environments by replacing the dependency on external assistive technologies, which are frequently blocked by lockdown security protocols with a purpose-built, self-voicing examination application. This section details the systematic plan used to gather stakeholder requirements, analyse the existing challenges of the Security-Accessibility Paradox, and design a robust solution that integrates multilingual Text-to-Speech (TTS), Braille display output, and kiosk security within a single desktop application. It serves as the technical blueprint of the project, laying out the tools and strategies that ensure the developed system is scientifically sound, inclusive by design, and scalable to other examination contexts in Nigeria and beyond.
P185 [Heading 2]: 3.2 Research Methodology
P186 [Normal]: The methodology adopted for the development of the Personalized Multilingual Examination System is the Agile Iterative Development Model. Unlike traditional sequential (waterfall) models that require all requirements to be finalized before any development begins, the Agile Iterative model allows for the creation of functional system components through repeated development cycles, known as iterations, enabling continuous stakeholder feedback and progressive refinement.
P187 [Normal]: This approach is particularly suited for this project for two critical reasons. First, the system’s core challenge: resolving the conflict between kiosk-mode security and operating system accessibility APIs is a high-risk technical problem whose solution cannot be fully specified in advance. Iterative development allows this component to be built, tested with visually impaired participants, and refined early in the project lifecycle before less complex features are developed. Second, the multilingual TTS engine requires continuous evaluation against language-specific accuracy benchmarks across English, Yoruba, Hausa, and Igbo. An iterative cycle allows diacritical rendering and phoneme accuracy to be validated in each language independently before integration.
