P194 [Heading 2]: 3.3 Detailed Functional Analysis of the System Architecture
P195 [Normal]: This section provides a granular explanation of the functional components that constitute the Personalized Multilingual Examination System. The system is designed to facilitate a seamless, independently navigable examination experience from candidate login through final submission, with all output available via audio, Braille, or a combination of both modalities. The architecture resolves the Security-Accessibility Paradox by internalising all accessibility functionality within the application itself, eliminating the dependency on external screen readers that lockdown browsers typically block.
P196 [Heading 3]: 3.3.1 Input Subsystems
P197 [Normal]: Administrator Panel: This serves as the oversight module, accessible only through a password-authenticated login separate from the candidate portal. It enables system administrators to upload examination questions in QTI 3.0 XML or structured CSV format, register candidates and assign them a language profile and examination session, export post-examination results as a CSV report with per-candidate scores and time-per-question analytics, and monitor active sessions for integrity events flagged by the audit log.
P198 [Normal]: Candidate Login Interface: This is the authentication gateway through which visually impaired candidates access the system. The interface is fully self-voicing from the first screen,  all labels, error messages, and confirmation prompts are read aloud using the built-in TTS engine. Candidates authenticate using their assigned candidate ID, after which the system loads their saved profile (preferred language, modality, speech rate, font size, and contrast theme) and routes them to the examination delivery engine.
P199 [Normal]: Setup Wizard (First-Run Personalization): On first launch, the system presents a guided configuration wizard that reads each option aloud before the candidate responds. The wizard captures the candidate’s preferred examination language (English, Yoruba, Hausa, or Igbo), output modality (audio, Braille display, or both), speech rate (0.5× to 2.0× default), visual font size (14pt to 36pt for low-vision users), and colour contrast theme (Light, Dark, or High-Contrast Yellow-on-Black). The resulting profile is encrypted and stored in a local SQLCipher database, persisting across all future examination sessions.
P202 [Heading 3]: 3.3.2 The Intelligence and Output Core
P203 [Normal]: Self-Voicing TTS Engine: This component is the primary output mechanism for blind candidates. Unlike systems that rely on external screen readers such as NVDA or JAWS which are blocked by kiosk security protocols, the TTS engine is embedded directly within the application and operates independently of any external process. It is built on eSpeak-NG (version 1.51+), an open-source, offline-capable synthesis engine that supports all four target languages. A critical sub-component, the Diacritics Pre-processor, normalises tone-marked Yoruba characters (e.g., ẹ́, ọ̀), hatted Hausa characters (ẗ, ẗ), and dot-below Igbo characters (ị, ọ) before handing them to the synthesis pipeline, ensuring correct tonal vocalization that the base engine would otherwise mishandle.
P204 [Normal]: Braille Display Output Module: This module translates examination text into Unified English Braille (UEB) using liblouis, the industry-standard open-source Braille translation library, and routes output to a connected refreshable Braille display via USB or Bluetooth HID. The module supports 40-cell and 80-cell display configurations, and enables candidates to pan through Braille output using the display’s routing keys. When no Braille hardware is detected at session start, the module falls back gracefully to audio-only mode without interrupting the candidate’s workflow.
P205 [Normal]: Encrypted Local Database (SQLite + SQLCipher): The database acts as the central persistent memory of the system. It stores candidate profiles, examination question banks loaded from administrator uploads, real-time session state (current question index, answers submitted, flags for review, time elapsed), and the post-session audit log. All database content is encrypted at rest using the SQLCipher extension, which applies AES-256 encryption transparently at the page level. This ensures that even if the examination device is physically accessed between sessions, raw examination content and candidate responses cannot be extracted without the application-managed decryption key.
P206 [Heading 3]: 3.3.3 The Examination Delivery Engine
P207 [Normal]: Question Delivery and Navigation: This component manages the presentation of examination questions to the candidate. It loads questions from the encrypted database, applies the configured randomization order, renders each question in the accessible UI, and routes the full question text including all answer options to both the TTS engine and the Braille output module simultaneously. Keyboard navigation is the sole interaction modality: arrow keys move between options, Page Down advances to the next question, the “R” key replays the current question from the beginning, and the “F” key flags a question for later review.
P208 [Normal]: Timer and Alert Subsystem: The timer enforces the configurable examination time limit and triggers audio countdown announcements at 15 minutes, 5 minutes, and 1 minute remaining. These alerts are injected directly into the TTS queue with higher priority than the current question, ensuring the candidate receives time warnings even mid-reading. The system performs an automatic local save of all submitted responses every 60 seconds, ensuring no data loss in the event of an unexpected shutdown.
P209 [Normal]: Submission Handler: When a candidate initiates submission, the handler first announces a confirmation prompt that includes a count of unanswered questions (e.g., “You have 3 unanswered questions. Do you wish to submit?”). Upon confirmation, all responses are encrypted using AES-256-GCM, written to the local database, and the session is closed. The audit log entry for submission includes a timestamp and an HMAC checksum of the response payload to ensure tamper-evidence.
P210 [Heading 3]: 3.3.4 The Security Module
P211 [Normal]: Kiosk Mode Engine: This component enforces examination security by entering a kiosk mode on exam start that prevents access to other applications, the desktop environment, and the file system. On Windows, it intercepts and blocks system key combinations including Alt+Tab, Ctrl+Alt+Del, and the Windows key using low-level keyboard hooks. On Linux, it achieves equivalent isolation through X11 window manager overrides. Critically, the kiosk mode is implemented at the application level rather than the OS process level, which allows it to selectively preserve access to the Windows Accessibility APIs (MSAA, IAccessible2, and UI Automation on Windows; AT-SPI on Linux) that the internal TTS and Braille modules rely on the precise capability that commercial lockdown browsers fail to offer.
P212 [Normal]: Audit Log: The audit log records all significant session events, including session start and end timestamps, navigation events, suspicious key combination attempts, system alert triggers, and submission confirmation. The log uses append-only file semantics, and each entry includes an HMAC checksum computed from the prior entry’s checksum and the current entry’s content, creating a chain that detects any retrospective tampering.
P213 [Heading 2]: 3.4 Procedural Flow and System Architecture Design
P214 [Normal]: The platform adopts a Three-Tier Architecture to ensure high performance, data integrity, and a clean separation of concerns. This structural pattern ensures that the complex security and TTS logic does not interfere with the user interface layer, and that the data persistence layer can be upgraded independently as institutional requirements evolve.
P217 [Normal]: Figure 3.2: Three-Tier System Architecture for the Personalized Multilingual Examination System
P219 [Heading 3]: 3.4.1 Presentation Layer (Client Tier)
P220 [Normal]: This is the user-facing interface, developed using Python with the PyQt6 framework. PyQt6 is selected over alternative Python GUI toolkits because it renders using native OS widget components, which ensures that the application’s interface elements are exposed through the operating system’s accessibility APIs by default a requirement for the internal TTS engine to correctly announce focus changes and state transitions. The presentation layer encompasses three primary interfaces:
P221 [List Paragraph]: Candidate Interface: Renders questions in accessible large-text format with high-contrast theming; manages keyboard focus traversal; relays all focus-change events to the TTS engine for immediate audio feedback.
P222 [List Paragraph]: Setup Wizard Interface: Guides first-time configuration of language, modality, speech rate, font size, and contrast theme; previews each setting with a live audio sample.
P223 [List Paragraph]: Administrator Dashboard: Provides question upload, candidate registration, and results export functionality; protected by a bcrypt-hashed password (cost factor ≥ 12) distinct from candidate credentials.
P224 [Heading 3]: 3.4.2 Application Layer (Logic Tier)
P225 [Normal]: This layer acts as the middleware between the presentation interface and the data tier, processing all examination logic and routing output to the appropriate delivery channel. It contains five core components:
P226 [List Paragraph]: TTS and Braille Orchestrator: Receives text events from the presentation layer and routes them concurrently to the eSpeak-NG synthesis pipeline and the liblouis Braille translation pipeline. Manages speech rate adjustments and language switching between questions.
P227 [List Paragraph]: Exam Engine: Controls the question delivery sequence, enforces randomization, manages the timer, processes keyboard navigation events, and triggers the auto-save cycle every 60 seconds.
P228 [List Paragraph]: Security Manager: Activates and deactivates kiosk mode, intercepts system key combinations, monitors for unauthorized process attachment attempts, and writes all security events to the tamper-evident audit log.
P229 [List Paragraph]: Diacritics Pre-processor: Normalises tone marks and special characters in Yoruba, Hausa, and Igbo question text before synthesis to ensure phonetically accurate audio output.
P230 [List Paragraph]: Profile Manager: Reads and writes candidate preferences from the encrypted SQLCipher database and injects the active profile settings into all other application layer components at session initialization.
P231 [Heading 3]: 3.4.3 Data Layer (Database Tier)
P232 [Normal]: This tier provides persistent encrypted storage and serves as the application’s memory across sessions. All data at this tier is encrypted at rest using SQLCipher (AES-256) and is never written to disk in plaintext.
P233 [List Paragraph]: Candidate Profiles Table: Stores encrypted candidate ID, preferred language, output modality, speech rate, font size, contrast theme, and a flag indicating whether the setup wizard has been completed.
P234 [List Paragraph]: Question Bank Table: Stores imported examination questions with their type (MCQ, True/False, Short Answer), language tag, option set, and correct answer for post-session scoring.
P235 [List Paragraph]: Session State Table: Records real-time examination progress: current question index, submitted answers, flagged questions, and elapsed time, updated every 60 seconds by the auto-save mechanism.
P236 [List Paragraph]: Audit Log Store: Append-only encrypted log with HMAC chaining, queried by the administrator panel for post-session integrity verification.
P237 [Heading 2]: 3.5 System Modelling
P238 [Normal]: System modelling involves the use of graphical representations to describe the functional requirements and the behavioural flow of the system. For this research, the Unified Modeling Language (UML) is employed to visualize how different users interact with the examination system, how data flows through the application during a live session, and how the system’s internal components relate to one another at a class level.
P239 [Heading 3]: 3.5.1 Use Case Diagram
P240 [Normal]: The use case diagram defines the system’s scope and the high-level functionalities available to each stakeholder. It illustrates the interactions between the three actors: the Visually Impaired Candidate, the Administrator, and the System itself (as an automated actor) and the core services provided by the platform.
P243 [Normal]: Figure 3.3: Use Case Diagram for the Personalized Multilingual Examination System
P245 [Normal]: As depicted in Figure 3.3, the actors and their respective use cases are defined as follows:
P246 [Normal]: Visually Impaired Candidate:
P247 [List Paragraph]: Configure Personalization Profile: Access the setup wizard on first launch to select preferred language, output modality, speech rate, font size, and contrast theme. Profile persists to local encrypted storage for all future sessions.
P248 [List Paragraph]: Authenticate and Begin Examination: Log in using assigned candidate ID; the system loads the saved profile and initiates the examination session with audio confirmation.
P249 [List Paragraph]: Navigate Questions via Keyboard: Use arrow keys, Page Down/Up, and shortcut keys to move between questions and answer options; receive audio and Braille feedback on every navigation event.
P250 [List Paragraph]: Replay and Flag Questions: Press “R” to replay the current question from the beginning; press “F” to flag a question for later review and return to it before final submission.
P251 [List Paragraph]: Submit Examination: Initiate submission; receive an audio prompt announcing the count of unanswered questions; confirm to encrypt and finalise the session.
P253 [Normal]: Administrator:
P254 [List Paragraph]: Upload Examination Questions: Import a QTI 3.0 XML or structured CSV question bank through the administrator panel; the system validates question format before storing to the encrypted database.
P255 [List Paragraph]: Register Candidates: Create candidate accounts, assign language profiles, and link candidates to examination sessions; credentials are stored using bcrypt hashing.
P256 [List Paragraph]: Export Session Results: Generate a CSV report of candidate scores, per-question timings, and session audit summaries for post-examination review.
P257 [List Paragraph]: Review Audit Log: Access the tamper-evident session audit log to verify examination integrity and investigate any flagged security events.
P259 [Normal]: System (Automated Actor):
P260 [List Paragraph]: Auto-Save Session State: Automatically persists candidate responses to the encrypted local database every 60 seconds to prevent data loss from unexpected shutdowns.
P261 [List Paragraph]: Announce Time Warnings: Inject audio countdown alerts at 15-minute, 5-minute, and 1-minute thresholds without interrupting the candidate’s reading flow.
P262 [List Paragraph]: Enforce Kiosk Security: Activate lockdown mode at examination start; log all security events to the audit log throughout the session.
P263 [Heading 2]: 3.6 System Flowchart
P264 [Normal]: The system flowchart provides a graphical representation of the platform’s operational logic. It visualizes the data pathways and decision points from initial system launch through candidate profile loading, active examination navigation, and final session submission. The flow is structured to reflect the experience of a blind candidate operating the system entirely through keyboard and audio feedback, with no visual dependency at any decision node.
P267 [Normal]: Figure 3.4: Logical Flow of the Personalized Multilingual Examination System
P269 [Normal]: As illustrated in Figure 3.4, the procedural logic is divided into four primary phases:
P270 [Heading 3]: 3.6.1 System Launch and Profile Resolution
P271 [Normal]: The process begins at the Start node when the candidate launches the application. The system immediately checks the local encrypted database for an existing candidate profile.
P272 [List Paragraph]: If no profile is found (first run), the Setup Wizard is launched, reads configuration options aloud, collects preferences, and persists the new profile to the encrypted database before proceeding to authentication.
P273 [List Paragraph]: If a profile exists, the system proceeds directly to the Candidate Login interface, loading the stored language, modality, and display settings in advance of authentication.
P274 [Heading 3]: 3.6.2 Authentication and Session Initialisation
P275 [Normal]: At the login interface, the candidate enters their assigned candidate ID via keyboard. The system validates the ID against the encrypted candidate register in the database.
P276 [List Paragraph]: If authentication fails, an audio error message is announced and the candidate is prompted to retry. After three consecutive failures, the session is locked and an alert is written to the audit log.
P277 [List Paragraph]: On successful authentication, the system loads the candidate’s profile, initialises the TTS engine with the preferred language and speech rate, activates Braille output if a compatible display is detected, and enters Kiosk Mode. An audio confirmation (“Examination ready. Press Enter to begin.”) signals the candidate that the session is live.
P278 [Heading 3]: 3.6.3 Examination Navigation and Response Capture
P279 [Normal]: The examination engine loads the randomized question set and presents the first question. The full question text and all answer options are read aloud automatically on first display, then pushed to the Braille display simultaneously.
P280 [List Paragraph]: The candidate navigates using keyboard controls. Each navigation event triggers an immediate audio announcement: option text when moving between MCQ answers, question text on question change, and flag confirmation when marking a question for review.
P281 [List Paragraph]: The timer runs concurrently, injecting priority audio announcements at 15 minutes, 5 minutes, and 1 minute remaining. The auto-save mechanism writes the current session state to the encrypted database every 60 seconds.
P282 [List Paragraph]: A “Review Flagged” mode, accessible before submission, allows the candidate to navigate only through flagged questions, replaying and revising answers before finalising.
P283 [Heading 3]: 3.6.4 Submission and Session Closure
P284 [Normal]: Successful Submission: When the candidate initiates submission, the system announces a confirmation message including the count of unanswered questions. On confirmation, all responses are encrypted with AES-256-GCM, written to the local database, the session audit log is finalised with an HMAC checksum, and Kiosk Mode is deactivated. An audio confirmation (“Examination submitted successfully.”) signals session closure.
P285 [Normal]: Incomplete or Timed-Out Submission: If the examination timer expires, the system automatically submits all answered questions with an audio announcement, applies the same encryption and audit log finalisation steps, and exits kiosk mode. Questions left unanswered are recorded as omitted in the results export.
P286 [Heading 2]: 3.7 Entity-Relationship Diagram (ERD)
P287 [Normal]: The Entity-Relationship Diagram (ERD) is used to illustrate the logical structure of the system’s local database. It defines the essential entities, their attributes, and the relationships that enable the application to coordinate candidate authentication, examination delivery, response capture, and post-session reporting within a single encrypted SQLite database.
P290 [Normal]: Figure 3.5: Entity-Relationship Diagram (ERD) for the Personalized Multilingual Examination System
P292 [Normal]: As shown in Figure 3.5, the database is designed around five key entities:
P293 [Normal]: Candidates Entity: This table stores the profile for every registered candidate. It uses an auto-generated CandidateID as its primary key and includes attributes for PreferredLanguage (an enumeration of EN, YO, HA, IG), OutputModality (AUDIO, BRAILLE, BOTH), SpeechRate (a decimal multiplier), FontSize, ContrastTheme, and a FirstRunComplete boolean flag. The bcrypt-hashed PIN used for authentication is stored here as a separate attribute.
P294 [Normal]: Questions Entity: This entity stores the imported examination question bank. Each question record includes a QuestionID (primary key), SessionID (foreign key linking to the ExamSessions entity), QuestionType (MCQ, TRUE_FALSE, SHORT_ANSWER), QuestionText, LanguageTag, OptionSet (a JSON array for MCQ options), and CorrectAnswer. Questions are loaded from administrator-uploaded QTI XML or CSV files and stored encrypted in the database at import time.
P295 [Normal]: ExamSessions Entity: This entity represents a specific scheduled examination. It stores SessionID (primary key), SessionName, StartTime, DurationMinutes, and Status (PENDING, ACTIVE, CLOSED). The SessionID foreign key links both the Questions entity and the CandidateResponses entity to a common examination context, enabling the results export to aggregate responses across all candidates enrolled in the same session.
P296 [Normal]: CandidateResponses Entity: This is the primary transactional table that records individual candidate answers during an active examination session. Each record includes a ResponseID (primary key), CandidateID (foreign key), QuestionID (foreign key), SubmittedAnswer, IsFlagged (boolean indicating the candidate flagged this question for review), TimeSpentSeconds, and EncryptedPayload the AES-256-GCM ciphertext of the answer written at submission. This dual storage (plaintext for the active session, ciphertext at submission) allows the timer and auto-save mechanism to operate efficiently while ensuring the final response is cryptographically secured.
P297 [Normal]: AuditLog Entity: This specialized append-only table records all significant session events. Each entry stores LogID (primary key), CandidateID (foreign key), SessionID (foreign key), EventType (an enumeration: SESSION_START, QUESTION_NAV, SECURITY_ALERT, AUTOSAVE, SUBMISSION, SESSION_END), EventTimestamp, EventDetail (a text description), and HMACChecksum a keyed hash computed from the concatenation of the previous entry’s HMAC and the current entry’s content. This chaining structure means that any retrospective modification to a log entry invalidates all subsequent checksums, providing tamper evidence for post-session integrity review.
